WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.225 --> 00:00:02.100
YUFENG GUO: As machine
learning has taken off

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.980
in the last few years,
the increased demand

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.920
for specialized computing
resources for training

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.890
and predictions has led to
the development of Tensor

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.620
Processing Units, or TPUs.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.070
But where do they come from and
how do they work on the inside?

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.640
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.924 --> 00:00:25.304
[SPACESHIP NOISES]

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.660
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.660
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.240
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.990
we're going to begin
a two-part adventure

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.120
into the world of
high-performance computing

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.380
at Google.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.170
In the first episode, we'll
look at the original TPU,

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.150
its design, and
how it came to be

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.720
used in everything from Google
Photos to Google Translate.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.010
Then, in the second episode,
we'll talk about TPU V2 and V3

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.830
and learn about how they work
to enable the next generation

00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:58.955
of machine learning.

00:00:58.955 --> 00:01:01.400
The trouble with
modern computing

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.650
is that we keep wanting
more processing power.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.160
Unfortunately, we're beginning
to run into the very limits

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.520
of physics.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.623
At least, this is very true
for general purpose processors,

00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:13.790
which is what you might
find in your everyday laptop

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.210
or smartphone.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.720
The need for more specific
computational power

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.130
is what led to the first tensor
processing unit to be created.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.500
Understanding the original
designs of the TPU

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.110
and why it was built
that way can help us

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.630
as we look forwards
towards designing

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.580
machine-learning
architecture and software

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.592
systems in the future.

00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:38.980
The first tensor processing
unit was built as a PCI Express

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.230
expansion card,
and it was plugged

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.740
straight into existing server
racks in Google data centers.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.620
It had a 700
megahertz clock speed

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.500
and had 40 watts of
power consumption,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.250
and it's been in production
since 2015, powering things

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.740
like Search, Translate,
Photos, and of course,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.420
famously featured in the
AlphaGo match in South Korea.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.860
Not only was the TPU
V1 much more performant

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.280
than the existing CPUs
and GPUs of the time,

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.620
it importantly had a much higher
performance per watt of energy.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.670
These two effects combined
to make the first TPU

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.300
a very effective chip.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.980
The chip featured
an architecture that

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.230
was specific for deep learning.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.040
It utilized reduced
precision, a matrix processor,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.020
and a minimal design
to reduce overhead.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.610
Let's look at each
of these now in turn.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.610
Neural networks boil down
to a rich set of matrix

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.830
multiplications and additions.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.400
The team decided to reduce
the precision of the chip,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.610
using just 8-bit
integers rather than

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.180
the conventional 32-bit
floating point numbers

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.930
and using a technique called
quantization that could convert

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.500
or map 32-bit floating point
numbers to 8-bit integers.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.750
So this allowed significantly
more integer multiplier units

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.180
to be fit into a single chip.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.180
So, speaking of
which, let's talk

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.510
about that matrix processor.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.930
Conventional processing
systems read and write

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.280
to memory very often, sometimes
after every single operation.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.760
This is because they do
general purpose processing.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.940
So in order to support many
different kinds of operations,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.680
the hardware design must
necessarily be fairly generic.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.810
However, this leads
to bottlenecks

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.150
due to all of those memory
accesses back and forth.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:37.010
So what if we could build a
special purpose processor that

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.250
performed a much more
limited set of operations,

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.810
but did so more quickly?

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.340
This is what the TPU tries to
do with its matrix processor.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.150
It uses what's called a
systolic array in order

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:55.670
to do a large hard-wired matrix
calculation without memory

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.200
access.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.810
One analogy I've
seen used to describe

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.150
this is that a CPU
is like printing out

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.460
letters one by one,
stamping them out,

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.990
whereas a GPU is printing
out a whole line at a time,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:12.350
while a TPU, it's stamping
out entire pages at a time.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:15.410
The core of the TPU is
a huge systolic array,

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:20.360
performing up to 250,000
operations per clock cycle.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.110
Most of the chip is dedicated
to this matrix multiplication

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.480
followed by addition operations.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.380
Even though the clock speed is a
seemingly paltry 700 megahertz,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.630
it is performing a ton of
operations every clock cycle.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.670
And one final note
about all of this

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.740
is that it is important
to remember that in order

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.940
to achieve this design,
the TPU version one only

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.850
did predictions.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.610
Now, you can't get your
hands on the TPU V1

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.970
or even use it
directly, but every time

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.090
you use something like Google
Photos or Google Translate,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.680
there's probably a TPU
powering your search somewhere.

